# Target C-II — nested outer-train diameter profiles are injective

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only rigidity/reuse theorem  
**Scope:** canonical full-core C-II representative; applied below to the `t<=1.844` small-leaf rectangle  
**Dependencies:** canonical core mixed rigidity `rank R_{C union H}=2v-3`; exact O--O train shore/shear primitive; relative-interior full-support KKT stress; repaired small-leaf Pareto rectangle  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The live rectangle route leaves one explicit obstruction: the same actual diameter edge can cross many ordered outer-to-outer leaf cuts. The support cannot, however, remain unchanged across two distinct nested O--O train cuts. Mixed rigidity rules that out.

Let `tau_1,...,tau_m` be distinct outer-to-outer complete stopped trains whose Jordan shore cuts are nested. Choose one end region as root and orient the shore splits so that

\[
S_1\subset S_2\subset\cdots\subset S_m.
\tag{PI.1}
\]

For each `i`, let

\[
P_i:=\{h\in H: h\text{ has one endpoint in }S_i\text{ and one outside }S_i\}
\tag{PI.2}
\]

be the set of actual diameter edges crossing the train cut. Then

\[
\boxed{P_i\ne P_j\qquad(i\ne j).}
\tag{PI.3}
\]

Consequently, if `H_*` is the union of all diameter profiles on the chain,

\[
\boxed{|H_*|\ge \left\lceil{m-1\over2}\right\rceil.}
\tag{PI.4}
\]

More locally, every strip `S_{i+1}\setminus S_i` contains an endpoint of an actual diameter edge whose profile starts or ends there.

## 1. Diameter support is interval-valued on a nested chain

Fix an actual diameter edge `h=xy`. Because the shores in (PI.1) are nested, the set

\[
I_h:=\{i:h\in P_i\}
\]

is a possibly empty consecutive interval of indices. Moreover, if `h` crosses two cuts `i<j`, the same endpoint of `h` lies in `S_i` and `S_j`. Thus orienting `r_h` from the complementary shore into the root-oriented shore gives one fixed vector throughout `I_h`.

Hence the profile `P_i` can change between `i` and `i+1` only when at least one diameter interval starts or ends at that strip. Each actual diameter edge has at most two such interval endpoints. This is purely the tree/path order of nested cuts; it does not assume that the train centerlines are straight.

## 2. Equal profiles force equal oriented train directions

Assume for contradiction that two distinct nested cuts have the same profile,

\[
P_i=P_j=:P.
\tag{PI.5}
\]

For train `tau_i`, choose its unit cut-contact vector `u_i` by orienting every cut contact from the complementary shore into `S_i`. Let

\[
A_i:=\sum_{e\in E(\tau_i)}\alpha_e>0
\]

be its positive KKT contact mass. The exact shore resultant is

\[
A_i u_i=\sum_{h\in P_i}\beta_h r_h.
\tag{PI.6}
\]

Because the shores are nested and the profiles in (PI.5) agree, every `r_h` has the same orientation in the two equations. Therefore

\[
A_i u_i=\sum_{h\in P}\beta_h r_h=A_j u_j.
\tag{PI.7}
\]

Both `A_i,A_j` are positive and `u_i,u_j` are unit vectors. Thus

\[
\boxed{A_i=A_j,\qquad u_i=u_j=:u.}
\tag{PI.8}
\]

This step uses the full positive KKT vector resultant, not only two-sided sign bracing.

## 3. Equal profile and direction give identical diameter response columns

Differentiate the exact finite one-train shear at angle zero. For `tau_i` this gives a contact infinitesimal flex `w_i` which is zero on the complementary shore and has velocity `Ju_i` on `S_i`. Its train angular-rate vector is exactly the coordinate basis vector `e_i`: contacts of `tau_i` have angular rate one and every other contact has rate zero.

For a diameter edge `h=xy`, its active rigidity row applied to `w_i` is zero if `h notin P_i`. If `h in P_i` and `r_h` is oriented into `S_i`, it is

\[
r_h\cdot Ju_i
\tag{PI.9}
\]

(up to the common harmless normalization of the diameter row).

Equations (PI.5), (PI.8), and the consistent nested orientation therefore give

\[
R_H w_i=R_H w_j.
\tag{PI.10}
\]

Both are contact flexes, so `R_C(w_i-w_j)=0`. Together with (PI.10),

\[
\boxed{R_{C\cup H}(w_i-w_j)=0.}
\tag{PI.11}
\]

on the nonisolated positive-contact core.

Canonical ZR/ZF mixed rigidity gives

\[
\operatorname{rank}R_{C\cup H}=2v-3,
\]

so its right kernel consists only of the three Euclidean infinitesimal motions. But the train-rate vector of `w_i-w_j` is

\[
e_i-e_j.
\tag{PI.12}
\]

Translations have zero train-rate vector, while a global infinitesimal rotation has a constant train-rate vector. The vector `e_i-e_j` is neither zero nor constant. Therefore `w_i-w_j` is non-Euclidean, contradicting (PI.11). This proves (PI.3). No lower bound on an individual KKT coefficient is used.

## 4. Every adjacent strip has a diameter endpoint event

Since `P_i ne P_{i+1}` for every adjacent pair, at least one actual diameter interval starts or ends between those cuts. Equivalently, at least one diameter edge has an endpoint in the source-vertex strip

\[
S_{i+1}\setminus S_i.
\tag{PI.13}
\]

Indeed, if an edge crosses exactly one of the two nested cuts, one of its endpoints lies in their set difference.

There are `m-1` adjacent strips. Each diameter edge can start once and end once on the chain, so it can account for at most two of these strip events. Hence `2|H_*|>=m-1`, proving (PI.4).

This is the desired finite reuse bound. A diameter chord may still brace a long interval of leaf cuts, but a chain of distinct O--O train cuts cannot be supported by one unchanged proper diameter profile.

## 5. Quantitative consequence for the audited small-leaf rectangle

The hostile-audited Pareto theorem gives, at `t<=1.844`, a **consecutive nested** smallest-leaf block with the safe balanced choice

\[
m>0.00779D-o(D)
\tag{PI.14}
\]

and more than `0.00779D-o(D)` common center trains crossing every leaf cut. Applying (PI.4) gives

\[
\boxed{|H_*|>0.003895D-o(D)>0.00389D-o(D).}
\tag{PI.15}
\]

Thus the same live survivor contains

```text
>0.00779 D nested smallest-leaf O-O cuts,
>0.00779 D center trains crossing all of them,
>0.00389 D distinct actual diameter edges bracing their union,
and a diameter-endpoint event in every adjacent leaf strip.
```

The exact arithmetic for the safe decimals is checked by `research/verify_target_c_nested_outer_train_profile_injectivity.py`.

The later inverse-`D` phase-collapse theorem can be intersected with this structure, but it is not needed for the injectivity proof itself.

## 6. Scope and remaining obstruction

- The result uses the canonical mixed-rigidity rank only on the nonisolated contact core. Contact-isolated source points are not inserted into that matrix.
- The finite O--O shear is used only through its audited derivative and exact train-rate coordinate.
- Nestedness comes from one dual-tree backbone path. No false global stack of all same-phase trains is assumed.
- The theorem counts distinct **diameter edges**, not distinct diameter endpoints; one edge may create two strip events.
- It does not bound how long one diameter interval may persist, and the constant `0.00389` alone is far too small to close C-II by a raw diameter-edge count.

The new frontier is narrower: combine the forced diameter endpoint in every leaf strip with the common center grid and inverse-`D` phase width. Any closing argument must now explain how a near-triangular ordered grid can place actual diameter endpoints in every one of linearly many consecutive strips without either paying a macroscopic interface cost or admitting a source-preserving multi-train shear.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only diameter-profile injectivity and linear distinct-bracer floor; C-II and Level I remain open.
